Kaixiang Liu
Language: C++
Platform: Mac (primary), Windows (compatible via bundled wxWidgets)


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
To run:

Mac (requires Homebrew wxWidgets):
  1. Install wxWidgets if not already installed:
       brew install wxwidgets

  2. From the ImageDisplay_C++_cross_platform/ folder:
       cmake -B build
       cmake --build build

Windows:
  1. Open ImageDisplay_C++_cross_platform/ in VS Code
  2. Run: CMake: Configure
  3. Run: CMake: Build
  (Uses bundled wxWidgets in dependency/ folder)

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
USAGE

  ./myProgram InputImage M Q B

  InputImage  Path to a 512x512 .rgb image file
              (planar format: all R values, then G, then B)

  M           Mode:
                1 = Standard 8x8 fixed block DCT (JPEG-style)
                2 = Adaptive NxN block DCT (quadtree-based)

  Q           Quantization step: all DCT coefficients divided by 2^Q
                Must be >= 0, OR set to -1 to auto-compute from B

  B           Target bits per pixel (compressed .DCT file after zip)
                Must be > 0.0, OR set to -1.0 if Q is provided

  NOTE: Exactly one of Q or B must be -1. Both cannot be positive.

Example invocations (matching assignment examples):
  ./myProgram Example.rgb 1 0 -1      # No quantization, output = input
  ./myProgram Example.rgb 1 10 -1     # Heavy quantization, blocky output
  ./myProgram Example.rgb 2 1 -1      # Adaptive blocks, light quantization
  ./myProgram Example.rgb 1 -1 1.5    # Auto Q for 1.5 bpp (Mode 1)
  ./myProgram Example.rgb 2 -1 1.5    # Auto Q for 1.5 bpp (Mode 2)

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
KEYBOARD CONTROLS

  B / b       Toggle block boundary overlay on/off
              Draws red rectangles showing the NxN block tiling
              Works for both Mode 1 (uniform 8x8) and Mode 2 (variable NxN)

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
OUTPUT FILES

  <InputImage>.DCT    Binary file of quantized DCT coefficients
                      Used for compression evaluation via zip metric:
                        Compressed bpp = zip(file.DCT) / (width x height)

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
ALGORITHM DESCRIPTION

Mode 1 - Standard 8x8 DCT (JPEG-style):
  1. Divide each channel into non-overlapping 8x8 blocks
  2. Apply 2D DCT to each block (via separable 1D DCT on rows then columns)
  3. Quantize each coefficient: round(coeff / 2^Q)
  4. Decode: multiply by 2^Q, apply 2D IDCT, clamp to [0, 255]

Mode 2 - Adaptive NxN Quadtree DCT:
  The image is divided into variable-sized blocks using a quadtree algorithm.
  Valid block sizes: {2, 4, 8, 16, 32} (all powers of 2 as per specification)

  Quadtree Decision Algorithm:
    1. Start with 32x32 tiles covering the image
    2. For each tile, compute the pixel variance across all 3 RGB channels
       Variance = E[x^2] - E[x]^2  (measures pixel value spread)
    3. If max variance >= VARIANCE_THRESHOLD (500.0) AND block size > 2:
         Split into 4 equal quadrants of size N/2 and recurse
       Else:
         Keep this block at size N and encode it

  Why variance works as a splitting criterion:
    - Low variance = smooth region (uniform sky, flat walls) → large block
    - High variance = detailed region (edges, textures, faces) → small block
    - Large blocks in smooth regions preserve quality with fewer coefficients
    - Small blocks in detailed regions capture fine detail accurately

  Result on Lena_512_512.rgb (Q=1):
    2x2  blocks: 5632  (fine detail: hair, hat feather, facial features)
    4x4  blocks: 1696  (moderate detail: face contours, fabric texture)
    8x8  blocks:  632  (medium regions: shoulders, background transitions)
    16x16 blocks: 232  (smoother regions: background, skin areas)
    32x32 blocks: 110  (very smooth regions: plain background)

  Why Mode 2 beats Mode 1 at the same bits/pixel:
    Mode 1 wastes bits encoding high-frequency coefficients in smooth regions
    that are near-zero anyway. Mode 2 uses large blocks there, which have
    fewer total coefficients and compress better. The saved bits are
    redistributed to detailed regions via smaller blocks, improving quality.

Auto Q Search (when Q = -1):
  Iterates Q from 0 upward, encoding the full image at each Q value,
  saving a temporary .DCT file, zipping it, and measuring compressed bpp.
  Stops at the first Q where bpp <= target B.
  Formula: bpp = (zip_file_size_bytes * 8) / (width * height)

DCT Implementation:
  Uses the separable 2D DCT property: apply 1D DCT to all rows, then
  all columns. This is mathematically equivalent to the full 2D formula.
  Standard library <cmath> used for cos() and sqrt(). No external libraries.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
LIMITATIONS

1. Variance threshold is hardcoded at 500.0 (defined as VARIANCE_THRESHOLD
   in Encoder.h). This value was empirically determined using the Lena test
   image. A different threshold may perform better on other image types
   (e.g., high-contrast graphics, medical images). Future work could
   auto-tune this threshold based on image statistics.

2. Image size is fixed at 512x512 (defined as IMAGE_WIDTH/IMAGE_HEIGHT in
   Image.h). The assignment specifies a fixed size will be provided.

3. Edge blocks that extend beyond image boundaries are handled by clamping
   pixel coordinates to the nearest valid pixel. Small strips at the right
   and bottom edges may have slightly reduced accuracy.

4. The auto-Q search uses a linear scan from Q=0 upward. A binary search
   would be faster but the linear scan is simpler and reliable.

5. The entropy coding step (Huffman/Arithmetic coding) is not implemented
   as per the assignment specification. The zip utility is used as a
   proxy for measuring compression efficiency.

6. The system() calls for zip in the auto-Q search use Unix commands and
   will not work on Windows without modification.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
FILE STRUCTURE
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  src/
    Main.cpp      Entry point, argument parsing, wxWidgets display, B key
    Image.h/.cpp  Image read/write (.rgb planar format)
    DCT.h/.cpp    Forward and inverse 2D DCT (separable, standard library)
    Encoder.h/.cpp  Block splitting, DCT, quantization, .DCT file output
    Decoder.h/.cpp  Inverse quantization, IDCT, image reconstruction

  CMakeLists.txt  Build configuration (Mac: Homebrew wxWidgets,
                  Windows: bundled wxWidgets in dependency/)

